+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole
+number of seconds, like `90`, or one or more groups of an integer followed by a unit: `d` for days, `h` for hours, `m`
+for minutes, and `s` for seconds, like `1h30m`. Units are case-insensitive, each unit may appear at most once, and they
+must be given in that order without spaces. A {class}`~datetime.timedelta` passed as a default or from Python is used
+unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration())
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=1h30m"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=90"])
+    invoke(wait, args=["--timeout=30m1h"])
+```
+
